  "notes": "Site is a client-rendered SPA (React) at demo.mercury.com, Admin dashboard view. It was already in dark mode on load (html[data-theme=dark], no UI toggle used). Per-chunk CSS files are served from cdn.mercury.com (a different origin than demo.mercury.com) without CORS headers, so document.styleSheets[i].cssRules throws SecurityError for every chunk when read from the page's own JS context - this affected ~120 chunked component stylesheets. Worked around it by curling the main entry stylesheet (https://cdn.mercury.com/builds/067cdc840dd6d57b383e8a49f9b7945314c080e5/static/css/main-BuT6UPED.css) directly with a Referer header, which contains all @font-face rules and the full design-token (--ds-*) palette for both the default (light) :root block and the [data-theme=dark] override block. Mercury's design system defines semantic CSS custom properties (--ds-background-*, --ds-text-*, --ds-border-*, --ds-icon-*, --ds-data-visualization-*) with literal hex/rgba values baked in per theme at build time - there is no live re-theming via prefers-color-scheme, and no oklch/hsl usage. Typography: only two font families are ever loaded by the app (confirmed via document.fonts on /dashboard, /accounts, /transactions): 'Arcadia Text' (variable, weight axis 400-500, used for virtually everything - body, buttons, table cells, nav, money amounts with tabular-nums) and 'Arcadia Display' (variable, weight axis 380-500, used only for large page headings like h1). The stylesheet also declares @font-face rules for Roboto Mono, IO, NeueMontreal, Satisfy, and MICR Encoding, but document.fonts reported all of these as 'unloaded' on every page checked, meaning no visible element actually uses them - they were not downloaded. Because Arcadia Text/Display are variable fonts, one file per family covers the full weight range the UI uses, so only 2 files were needed rather than 3-6 static-weight files. Colors were cross-checked against live computed styles (getComputedStyle) on real elements: sidebar bg, card bg, search input bg, primary 'Send' button, table header/cell text, hover state of a sidebar link, and a red 'Failed' status badge on the Transactions page - all matched their corresponding --ds-* token values within rounding. The one exception is the browser's native focus outline on a text input, which showed the default text color rather than --ds-border-focus-ring (#8da4f5); the token value is reported here since it's the design system's documented focus color and likely applies to custom-styled focus states elsewhere in the app."
}